---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  formatNumber,
  HOME_KWH_PER_YEAR,
  KETTLE_KW,
  LOAD_FACTOR,
  LOAD_FACTOR_DISCLOSURE,
  WATER_LITRES_PER_PERSON_PER_DAY,
} from "../lib/units";
import { correctionsMailto } from "../lib/contact";
import { getConfig } from "../lib/sites";
import { AIGZ_ZONES } from "../lib/aigz";

const config = getConfig();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const replyMailto = correctionsMailto({ kind: "right-of-reply" });
const correctionMailto = correctionsMailto();
---

<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="How we evidence the physical footprint of AI and cloud infrastructure across Yorkshire, the Humber, and Teesside: stance, conversion factors, and right of reply.">
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl m-0 tracking-tight">Methodology</h1>
  <p class="text-[var(--muted)] mt-2 max-w-2xl">
    How we choose, label, and correct figures so the physical cost of regional AI and cloud infrastructure
    stays clear. This page is part of the site itself, not an appendix.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-12" id="inclusion">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">What we include</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      The map covers the regional compute corridor: very large campuses and other significant
      data centre proposals in Yorkshire, the Humber, and Teesside. That includes large brownfield and farmland
      schemes, and smaller heat-reuse sites where they change the local environmental picture (for example
      Deep Green Bradford). It is not limited to the biggest cloud operators alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="stance">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">How to read our choices</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      AI and cloud services can look weightless in software. On the ground they mean grid connections,
      land take, cooling water, and contested planning. This site exists to make that physical footprint
      across Yorkshire, the Humber, and Teesside visible and comparable. The angle is disclosed, not neutral.
      Within it the rules are strict: every number is sourced or null; claims are shown as claims;
      we let sourced figures and side-by-side comparisons make the case, and we avoid loaded adjectives.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-2xl">
      We lead with stated power size because that is what planning documents and press releases give, and because
      operators here do not publish load factor (what share of that peak they expect to use day to day).
      Comparison units (kettles, local geography, homes, water, land) are chosen so communities can feel the
      scale without a specialist glossary. Evidence that would change a figure: a planning document page,
      a metered disclosure, or a correction from a named operator via the right-of-reply channel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="evidence">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Evidence standard</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <li>Press releases are not evidence for quantitative site capacity unless corroborated by a planning document.</li>
      <li>“Sourced” means a specific document and page or paragraph, not merely “the planning portal”.</li>
      <li>A claim with no published data is labelled <code>no public data</code>.</li>
      <li>Campaign sources are labelled as campaign sources.</li>
      <li>No status above <code>proposed</code> without a resolving link to the live planning application.</li>
      <li>
        When we add a source, we should also save an archived copy.
        Gaps are listed on the <a href={`${base}/research`}>Research</a> page and in the data changelog.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="basis">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Where a figure came from</h2>
    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-3 max-w-2xl text-sm">
      <div><dt class="font-semibold">Operator</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[var(--muted)]">Operator or promoter figure, never presented as verified fact.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-semibold">Planning</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[var(--muted)]">Taken from a planning application document.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-semibold">Estimated</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[var(--muted)]">Derived with stated assumptions; shown as a range when more than one assumption is required.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="font-semibold">Measured</dt><dd class="m-0 text-[var(--muted)]">Metered or otherwise measured disclosure.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="power">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Stated size is not electricity use</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      When this site says <strong>Stated size</strong>, it means how large <em>that data-centre scheme</em> claims to be,
      in megawatts: usually the biggest size stated for that site, otherwise the grid connection applied for,
      otherwise the first stage only. The source for the chosen figure sits with the number on the site page and map panel.
      That is a size on paper for one site. It is not how much electricity the facility will draw while running, and it is not a
      regional total when two schemes publish the same figure.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">{LOAD_FACTOR_DISCLOSURE}</p>
    <ul class="max-w-2xl">
      <li><strong>Stated size</strong> (nameplate or contracted): fair for like-for-like size comparisons only (for example kettles). Measured in megawatts (MW).</li>
      <li><strong>Assumed average use</strong>: stated size × {Math.round(LOAD_FACTOR.low * 100)}-{Math.round(LOAD_FACTOR.high * 100)}% load factor. All energy comparisons use this, always as a range.</li>
      <li><strong>Measured energy</strong>: supersedes assumptions when available.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted)] max-w-2xl">{LOAD_FACTOR.source}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="units">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Conversion factors</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <li>
        <strong>Kettle</strong>: {KETTLE_KW} kilowatts continuous (like-for-like power comparison, not annual use).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Home</strong>: {formatNumber(HOME_KWH_PER_YEAR.value)} kilowatt-hours per year.
        Source: {HOME_KWH_PER_YEAR.source}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local geography</strong>: Department for Energy Security and Net Zero (DESNZ) subnational
        electricity consumption at local-authority level.
        Download the <a href={`${base}/data/local_consumption.json`}>local consumption figures</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Water</strong>: {WATER_LITRES_PER_PERSON_PER_DAY.value} litres per person per day.
        Source: {WATER_LITRES_PER_PERSON_PER_DAY.source}. Olympic swimming pool as a secondary unit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Land</strong>: acres to football pitches (Football Association pitch about 105×68 metres,
        roughly 1.76 acres). Wheat-foregone only where Agricultural Land Classification (ALC) grades support
        a yield comparison. Best and most versatile (BMV) farmland is grades 1, 2, and 3a.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drax biomass</strong>: pellet tonnes and forestry hectares as ranges.
        No tree counts in this version.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="circles">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Circle scaling</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Map circles scale by the best available power size for <em>that site</em>, in fixed field priority:
      biggest size stated for that site, then grid connection applied for, then first stage only.
      Each site page states which field was used and the source of that figure.
      If two sites publish the same megawatts, both keep that size (joint maximum); compare ranks them equal and lists them A-Z.
      Conflicting maxima in different documents: we record one figure with its source. We do not average.
      Map circle sizes follow the megawatt key in the legend, which squeezes the range so a 5.6&nbsp;MW
      site stays clickable beside a 1&nbsp;gigawatt (1,000&nbsp;MW) scheme. Do not treat circle area as
      proportional to megawatts on the map. Compare does use true proportions: twice the megawatts is twice the area.
      Sites with no disclosed megawatts render as dashed circles sized by site area (acres).
      If area is also unknown, a small dashed marker still appears at the published location
      (Eggborough today). That marks presence only, not a size claim.
      Colour encodes planning status with a colour-vision-friendly set
      (warm stone, purple, ochre, teal, blue) so consecutive stages stay in distant hues.
      Outline style (solid vs dashed) is separate: whether megawatts are disclosed.
      The compare view draws circles so area matches megawatts (twice the power looks twice as large).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="aigz">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">AI Growth Zone overlay</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      The optional overlay on the map shows where AI Growth Zones (AIGZ) are being sought across the region.
      None of them is designated. As of July 2026 the confirmed UK zones are Oxfordshire, the North East,
      North Wales, South Wales, and Scotland (see the
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/ai-growth-zones" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOV.UK register</a>);
      the four below are live bids. Each is drawn as an indicative circle around the bid's lead site, so the
      shaded area shows an approximate location rather than an official boundary.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-3 max-w-2xl space-y-2">
      {AIGZ_ZONES.map((z) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{z.name}</strong> (bid). {z.lead}. {z.note}{" "}
          <a href={z.source.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{z.source.title}</a>.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="right-of-reply">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Standing right of reply</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Any named operator or developer may respond to any figure or claim on this site.
      Responses are published in full on the relevant site page, with the date they were received.
      Send responses to <a href={replyMailto}>{config.contact.corrections_email}</a>.
      Suggested subject: “Yorkshire Compute Belt: right of reply”.
      Or open a <a href={config.contact.github_issues}>GitHub issue</a>.
      Site pages show “Right of reply: open; no response received” rather than silence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="corrections">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Corrections policy</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Material errors are corrected in the data file and noted in the repository’s
      <code>data/CHANGELOG.md</code> (visible before launch, updated with each correction).
      Prefer a planning-document page citation. Councillors and people in affected communities without
      a GitHub account can email <a href={correctionMailto}>{config.contact.corrections_email}</a>.
      Suggested subject: “Yorkshire Compute Belt: correction”.
      Nothing on the public site writes the data file directly; every tip is checked by editors first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="updates">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">How fresh the figures are</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Each site shows when we last reviewed its figures. If the typical review age across the map goes past
      {config.staleness_banner_days} days, a banner appears site-wide so outdated data is obvious, not quietly wrong.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12" id="claims">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Claim statuses</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      On each site page, operator claims carry a status about the public record: unverified, no public data,
      or no longer stated. That describes what is published, not a verdict that the claim is true or false.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Open data</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Sites ship as GeoJSON (<code>data/sites.geojson</code>), coordinates <code>[longitude, latitude]</code>.
      Schema versioned from day one. Region config lives in <code>data/config.json</code>.
      Download: <a href={`${base}/data/sites.geojson`}>sites.geojson</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/data/schema.json`}>schema</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/data/local_consumption.json`}>local consumption</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
